{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looped cadence—too rhythmic, too clean—and the sun rose exactly at 6:17 a.m., casting the same golden streak across his bedroom wall as it had for the past seven mornings. He rubbed his eyes, expecting sleep fog, but his fingers registered a faint pixelated shimmer, like static clinging to his skin. The coffee he brewed tasted algorithmically perfect—no bitterness, no inconsistency—and when he glanced at his phone, the time blinked 6:17 again, then again, then again, cycling in a loop. Outside, his neighbor waved at him with the same stiff, three-step motion, always smiling with exactly the same number of teeth showing. He ran to the window, screaming, and the world stuttered—a brief, glitchy freeze—before the neighbor resumed waving, oblivious. The air smelled faintly of compressed air and circuitry. He was no longer a man. He was a character in a simulation, and the game had no quit button.\n\n***\n\nHe tried to break it. He jumped off the roof. He walked into traffic. He screamed his name until his throat bled—though the blood, when he touched it, dissolved into glowing particles that vanished before hitting the ground. No one reacted. No one even noticed. Then, on the third day, he found a terminal hidden behind his bathroom mirror, its screen glowing with a single line of text: “Player 7392, you’ve reached the end of your tutorial. Would you like to unlock the next level?” He stared at it, trembling, and realized the game had been teaching him to accept his reality. He reached out, fingers hovering over the “Yes” button, and wondered if he’d ever been real to begin with—or if he was just the glitch the developers had forgotten to patch. The screen blinked once, then displayed: “Welcome to Level Two.” The birds began to chirp again.", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.61981076002120